### Migrate `experimental.bundlePagesExternals` to `bundlePagesRouterDependencies` in Next.js 15

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/v16.1.6/docs/01-app/02-guides/upgrading/version-15.mdx

This snippet illustrates the migration of the `experimental.bundlePagesExternals` configuration option to its stable counterpart, `bundlePagesRouterDependencies`, in `next.config.js`. This change stabilizes the bundling of external dependencies for Pages Router. Ensure your `next.config.js` is updated to reflect this new property.

```javascript
/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  // Before
  experimental: {
    bundlePagesExternals: true,
  },

  // After
  bundlePagesRouterDependencies: true,
}

module.exports = nextConfig
```

--------------------------------

### Migrate `experimental.serverComponentsExternalPackages` to `serverExternalPackages` in Next.js 15

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/v16.1.6/docs/01-app/02-guides/upgrading/version-15.mdx

This snippet shows how to update the `experimental.serverComponentsExternalPackages` configuration to the new stable `serverExternalPackages` property in `next.config.js`. This change stabilizes the configuration for external packages used in Server Components. Update your `next.config.js` to use the new property name.

```javascript
/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  // Before
  experimental: {
    serverComponentsExternalPackages: ['package-name'],
  },

  // After
  serverExternalPackages: ['package-name'],
}

module.exports = nextConfig
```

--------------------------------

### Deprecated Partial Pre-Rendering (PPR) Configuration in Next.js 15

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/v16.1.6/docs/01-app/02-guides/upgrading/version-16.mdx

This snippet displays the deprecated configuration for Partial Pre-Rendering (PPR) used in Next.js 15 canaries. Users currently employing `experimental.ppr: true` are advised to remain on their current Next.js 15 canary version until a migration guide to Cache Components is provided.

```js
/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  // If you are using PPR today
  // stay in the current Next.js 15 canary
  experimental: {
    ppr: true,
  },
}

module.exports = nextConfig
```

### Migrating from pages to app > Overview

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/v16.1.6/docs/01-app/02-guides/migrating/app-router-migration.mdx

The App Router represents a significant architectural shift in Next.js that introduces React features like Server Components and Suspense. Migrating to the App Router involves learning new concepts, mental models, and behavioral changes. To manage this complexity effectively, it is recommended to break down the migration into smaller, incremental steps. The `app` directory is intentionally designed to work simultaneously with the `pages` directory, allowing for page-by-page migration rather than requiring a complete rewrite of your application at once.

--------------------------------

### App Router > Migration

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/v16.1.6/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/05-config/01-next-config-js/appDir.mdx

The `appDir` option is no longer needed as of Next.js 13.4, as the App Router is now stable and the default approach for new Next.js projects. This means you no longer need to explicitly enable this feature in your configuration. If you're working with an older Next.js project using the `pages` directory, you can incrementally adopt the App Router by migrating your routes and components gradually.

